{"archive_version": "20260604.144757", "book_slug": "physics", "book_state": "live", "book_title": "Physics", "book_uuid": "cce64fde-f448-43b8-ae88-27705cceb0da", "book_version": "dfdfd7a", "caption": null, "content_url": "https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20260604.144757/contents/cce64fde-f448-43b8-ae88-27705cceb0da@dfdfd7a:e202e0db-9d73-4300-b3c7-20f022d60fd0.json", "context": null, "element_id": "fs-id1167064585090", "has_thead": false, "header_cells": [], "html": "<table id=\"fs-id1167064585090\" class=\"unnumbered unstyled section-key-terms\" data-summary=\"Section Key Terms is a three column table. The first row reads aberration, chromatic aberration, concave lens. The second row reads converging lens, convex lens, diverging lens. The third row reads  eyepiece, objective, ocular. The fourth row reads parfocal.\" data-label=\"\" role=\"presentation\"><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>aberration</td>\n<td>chromatic aberration</td>\n<td>concave lens</td>\n<td>converging lens</td>\n<td>convex lens</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>diverging lens</td>\n<td>eyepiece</td>\n<td>objective</td>\n<td>ocular</td>\n<td>parfocal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>", "id": "openstax/physics/16-3-lenses/tab-000", "index_on_page": 0, "is_layout": true, "kind": "table", "license_name": "Creative Commons Attribution License", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "n_cols": 5, "n_rows": 2, "page_slug": "16-3-lenses", "page_title": "16.3 Lenses", "page_uuid": "e202e0db-9d73-4300-b3c7-20f022d60fd0", "retrieved_at": "2026-10-08", "role": "presentation", "source": "openstax", "source_url": "https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/16-3-lenses#fs-id1167064585090", "summary": "Section Key Terms is a three column table. The first row reads aberration, chromatic aberration, concave lens. The second row reads converging lens, convex lens, diverging lens. The third row reads  eyepiece, objective, ocular. The fourth row reads parfocal.", "table_number": null}
{"archive_version": "20260604.144757", "book_slug": "physics", "book_state": "live", "book_title": "Physics", "book_uuid": "cce64fde-f448-43b8-ae88-27705cceb0da", "book_version": "dfdfd7a", "caption": "Three Types of Images Formed by Lenses", "content_url": "https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20260604.144757/contents/cce64fde-f448-43b8-ae88-27705cceb0da@dfdfd7a:e202e0db-9d73-4300-b3c7-20f022d60fd0.json", "context": null, "element_id": "Table_16_03", "has_thead": true, "header_cells": [{"col": 0, "row": 0, "scope": "col", "text": "Type"}, {"col": 1, "row": 0, "scope": "col", "text": "Formed When"}, {"col": 2, "row": 0, "scope": "col", "text": "Image Type"}, {"col": 3, "row": 0, "scope": "col", "text": "di"}, {"col": 4, "row": 0, "scope": "col", "text": "M"}], "html": "<table id=\"Table_16_03\" data-summary=\"Table with five columns and four rows. The top left cell reads “Type,” with cells to the right reading, respectively, “Formed When,” “Image Type,” “di,” and “m.” The next two rows describe type “case 1” and “case 2” placement of the object outside and within the positive focal length (convex lens), respectively, with the image produced being either real, having a positive image distance, and having a negative magnification (inverted image); or being virtual, having a negative image distance, and having a positive magnification (erect image) greater than one. Case 3 involves a negative focal length (concave lens), so the image type is virtual, the image distance is negative, and the magnification is positive (an erect image) and less than one.\"><thead>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"col\">Type</th>\n<th scope=\"col\">Formed When</th>\n<th scope=\"col\">Image Type</th>\n<th scope=\"col\"><em data-effect=\"italics\">d</em><sub>i</sub></th>\n<th scope=\"col\"><em data-effect=\"italics\">M</em></th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Case 1</td>\n<td><em data-effect=\"italics\">f </em>positive, <em data-effect=\"italics\">d<sub>o</sub></em> &gt; <em data-effect=\"italics\">f</em></td>\n<td>Real</td>\n<td>Positive</td>\n<td>Negative <em data-effect=\"italics\">m</em> &gt;, &lt;, or = ‒1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Case 2</td>\n<td><em data-effect=\"italics\">f </em>positive, <em data-effect=\"italics\">d<sub>o</sub></em> &lt; <em data-effect=\"italics\">f</em></td>\n<td>Virtual</td>\n<td>Negative</td>\n<td>Positive <em data-effect=\"italics\">m</em> &gt; 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Case 3</td>\n<td><em data-effect=\"italics\">f </em>negative</td>\n<td>Virtual</td>\n<td>Negative</td>\n<td>Positive <em data-effect=\"italics\">m</em> &lt; 1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>", "id": "openstax/physics/16-3-lenses/tab-001", "index_on_page": 1, "is_layout": false, "kind": "table", "license_name": "Creative Commons Attribution License", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "n_cols": 5, "n_rows": 4, "page_slug": "16-3-lenses", "page_title": "16.3 Lenses", "page_uuid": "e202e0db-9d73-4300-b3c7-20f022d60fd0", "retrieved_at": "2026-10-08", "role": null, "source": "openstax", "source_url": "https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/16-3-lenses#Table_16_03", "summary": "Table with five columns and four rows. The top left cell reads “Type,” with cells to the right reading, respectively, “Formed When,” “Image Type,” “di,” and “m.” The next two rows describe type “case 1” and “case 2” placement of the object outside and within the positive focal length (convex lens), respectively, with the image produced being either real, having a positive image distance, and having a negative magnification (inverted image); or being virtual, having a negative image distance, and having a positive magnification (erect image) greater than one. Case 3 involves a negative focal length (concave lens), so the image type is virtual, the image distance is negative, and the magnification is positive (an erect image) and less than one.", "table_number": "16.3"}
{"archive_version": "20260604.144757", "book_slug": "physics", "book_state": "live", "book_title": "Physics", "book_uuid": "cce64fde-f448-43b8-ae88-27705cceb0da", "book_version": "dfdfd7a", "caption": "Refractive Indices Relevant to the Eye", "content_url": "https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20260604.144757/contents/cce64fde-f448-43b8-ae88-27705cceb0da@dfdfd7a:e202e0db-9d73-4300-b3c7-20f022d60fd0.json", "context": null, "element_id": "Table_16_04", "has_thead": true, "header_cells": [{"col": 0, "row": 0, "scope": "col", "text": "Material"}, {"col": 1, "row": 0, "scope": "col", "text": "Index of Refraction"}], "html": "<table id=\"Table_16_04\" data-summary=\"Table with two columns and seven rows. The top left cell reads “Material”; the top right cell reads “Index of Refraction.” The next six rows describe the various transparent substances of which the human eye is composed and the index of refraction of that substance.\"><thead>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"col\">Material</th>\n<th scope=\"col\">Index of Refraction</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Water</td>\n<td>1.33</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Air</td>\n<td>1.00</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cornea</td>\n<td>1.38</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aqueous humor</td>\n<td>1.34</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lens</td>\n<td>1.41 average<sup>*</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vitreous humor</td>\n<td>1.34</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody><tfoot>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\"><sup>*</sup>The index of refraction varies throughout the lens and is greatest at its center.</td>\n</tr>\n</tfoot></table>", "id": "openstax/physics/16-3-lenses/tab-002", "index_on_page": 2, "is_layout": false, "kind": "table", "license_name": "Creative Commons Attribution License", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "n_cols": 2, "n_rows": 8, "page_slug": "16-3-lenses", "page_title": "16.3 Lenses", "page_uuid": "e202e0db-9d73-4300-b3c7-20f022d60fd0", "retrieved_at": "2026-10-08", "role": null, "source": "openstax", "source_url": "https://openstax.org/books/physics/pages/16-3-lenses#Table_16_04", "summary": "Table with two columns and seven rows. The top left cell reads “Material”; the top right cell reads “Index of Refraction.” The next six rows describe the various transparent substances of which the human eye is composed and the index of refraction of that substance.", "table_number": "16.4"}
